  "details": "Some languages in average have longer phrases comparing to other languages, for various reasons such as glyph size, importance of suffixes and endings, or simply longer words for same meaning.\n\nBecause of this some phrases may not fit into labels, even when it is multiline.\n\nTo fit text we had to reduce font size.\n\nBut in many places of UI font **already too small** relative to icons, and **hard to read** from far, older HMDs like HTC Vive or with decreased VR resolution.\n\nPossible **solution** may be use of (Semi-)**Condensed Fonts**, the same way it was made in some other projects, like Minecraft. \n\nThat (Semi-)Condensed setting might be **per-language**, leaving languages like English with **Regular** fonts as-is, but leaving more space for glyphs in other languages where it is needed. (Semi-)**Condensed** variant might be applied **application-wide** without deeply looking into each UI element, keeping entire UI of VRChat in same style.\n\nBelow you can find an example of a problem:\n\n*User* in Russian is *Пользователь*, and it cannot be easily re-phrased or broken into few lines. This is atomic word.\n\nAnother example: In Minecraft thinner font allows **more characters** to be placed on the signs. But in Minecraft it is not really *condensed*, it is just *thinner*.\n\nWe can use real-condensed fonts - below another example with Regular and Semi-Condensed variants of DeJaVu Sans Bold. All settings are the same, font looks exactly the same just second variant being a bit **compressed horizontally**.",
